🚀 Daily Git Commands I Use as a DevOps Cloud Engineer 🚀

As part of my daily tasks, I rely on Git for version control, collaboration, and managing project workflows.
 Here are the Git commands I use regularly 👇

🔹 1. Clone a repository
 git clone https://lnkd.in/dFfrRA8M

🔹 2. Check current branch and repo status

* git branch

* git status

* git remote -v → Check which remote repo is connected

🔹 3. Create or switch branches

* git checkout -b feature-branch → Create and switch to a new branch

* git checkout main → Switch to main branch

🔹 4. Undo local changes in a file

* git checkout <file-path> → Revert any local changes made to a file

🔹 5. Check differences

* git diff <file-path> → View local changes in a specific file

* git diff <commit-id> → See what changes were made in a specific commit

🔹 6. Tag a commit (for safe rollback)

* git tag <tag-name> → Mark a specific point in history

🔹 7. Stage and commit changes

* git add . → Stage all changes

* git commit -m "Your commit message" → Commit with a message

🔹 8. Push and pull changes

* git push → Push changes to remote

* git pull → Pull latest changes from remote

🔹 9. View commit history

* git log → See commit history for the current branch

🔹 10. Merge changes from another branch

* git merge <branch-name> → Merge a feature branch into your current branch

🔹 11. Resolve merge conflicts

* Open the conflicted file using vi <file-name>

* Use dd in Vim to delete conflict lines

* Then run:

git add .
 git commit -m "Resolved conflicts"

🔹 12. Manage remote access

* Use git remote -v to verify connected remotes

* Restrict or allow branch push access to development teams as needed
